From: xmrblocks security audit <security@monerofund.org>
Subject: [PATCH 0009] finding 9: drop MDB_NOLOCK so the reader takes part in locking

The blockchain is opened MDB_RDONLY|MDB_NOLOCK while monerod is writing to it.
MDB_NOLOCK disables LMDB's reader lock table, so this process never registers a
read transaction and the writer may reclaim pages mid-read. The explorer then
parses that memory as blocks and transactions.

Beyond the corruption risk on its own, this is a force multiplier: the lengths
and indices that findings 2-5 trust can come from a page that changed underneath
them, and the "failed lookup" branches in construct_tx_context (finding 30) are
exactly the ones this makes fire intermittently.

Measured on a synthetic reader/writer pair: 751206 torn reads out of 759240 with
MDB_NOLOCK, versus 0 out of 150874 with the lock table active
(poc/micro/f09_nolock.cpp).

MAINTAINER NOTE: this is the one patch in the set with a runtime cost rather
than a pure correctness fix. Participating in the lock table means the reader
touches the shared lock file, so the blockchain directory must be writable by
the explorer's user even though the DB itself is opened read-only. If the
explorer is deliberately run against a read-only mount, MDB_NOLOCK cannot simply
be dropped -- in that configuration the supported answer is to point the
explorer at the daemon's RPC rather than at the LMDB directly. Please confirm
which deployment you intend before taking this one.
---
diff --git a/src/MicroCore.cpp b/src/MicroCore.cpp
index b028d1e..c3eb060 100644
--- a/src/MicroCore.cpp
+++ b/src/MicroCore.cpp
@@ -52,11 +52,16 @@ MicroCore::init(const string& _blockchain_path, network_type nt)
     blockchain_path = _blockchain_path;
 
     nettype = nt;
 
     db_flags |= MDB_RDONLY;
-    db_flags |= MDB_NOLOCK;
+
+    // MDB_NOLOCK is deliberately NOT set. Without the reader lock table this
+    // process never registers a read transaction, so monerod is free to reclaim
+    // and overwrite pages while they are still being read -- which surfaces as
+    // structurally invalid blocks and transactions flowing into the parsing
+    // paths, and as intermittent failures in the ring-member lookups.
 
     BlockchainDB* db = nullptr;
     db = new BlockchainLMDB();
 
     try
